.SH "react-remove-scroll"
.PP
\fR\fP
.PP
Disables scroll outside of \fRchildren\fP node\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
🖱 mouse and touch devices friendly
.IP "\(bu" 2
📈 vertical and horizontal
.IP "\(bu" 2
📜 removes document scroll bar maintaining it space
.IP "\(bu" 2
✅ support nested scrollable elements
.IP "\(bu" 2
🕳 supports react-portals (uses React Event system)
.IP "\(bu" 2
☠️ it could block literally any scroll anywhere
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
Just wrap content, which should be scrollable, and everything else would not\&. 
.PP
.nf
import {RemoveScroll} from 'react\-remove\-scroll';

<RemoveScroll>
  Only this content would be scrollable
</RemoveScroll>  

.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRRemoveScroll\fP accept following props
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchildren\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[enabled]\fP - activate or deactivate component behaviour without removing it\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[allowPinchZoom=false]\fP - enabled "pinch-n-zoom" behavior\&. By default it might be prevented\&. However - pinch and zoom might break "scroll isolation", and \fBdisabled by default\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[noRelative=false]\fP - prevents setting \fRposition: relative\fP on the body\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[noIsolation=false]\fP - disables outer event capturing\&. Event capturing is React friendly and unlikely be a problem\&. But if you are using \fIshadowbox\fP of some sort - you dont need it\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[inert=false]\fP - ☠️(be careful) disables events the rest of page completely using \fRpointer-events\fP except the Lock(+shards)\&. React portals not friendly, might lead to production issues\&. Enable only for \fBrare\fP cases, when you have to disable scrollbars somewhere on the page(except body, Lock and shards)\&. 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[forwardProps]\fP - will forward all props to the \fRchildren\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[className]\fP - className for an internal div
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[removeScrollBar]\fP - to control scroll bar removal\&. Set to false, if you prefer to keep it (wheel and touch scroll is still disabled)\&.
.PP
.SH "Size"
.PP
.SS "- (🧩 full) 1\&.7kb after compression (excluding tslib)\&."
.IP "\(bu" 2
(👁 UI) \fB400b\fP, visual elements only
.IP "\(bu" 2
(🚗 sidecar) 1\&.5kb, side effects 
.PP
.nf
import {sidecar} from "react\-remove\-scroll";
import {RemoveScroll} from 'react\-remove\-scroll/UI';

const sidecar = sidecar(() => import('react\-remove\-scroll/sidecar'));

<RemoveScroll sideCar={sidecar}>
  Will load logic from a sidecar when needed
</RemoveScroll>  

.fi
.PP

.PP

.PP
.RS 4
Consider setting \fR-webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;\fP on a document level for a proper mobile experience\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "Internal div"
By default RemoveScroll will create a div to handle and capture events\&. You may specify \fRclassName\fP for it, if you need, or \fBremove it\fP\&.

.PP
The following code samples will produce the same output 
.PP
.nf
<RemoveScroll className="scroll">
  Only this content would be scrollable
</RemoveScroll>

.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
<RemoveScroll forwardProps>
  <div className="scroll"> //RemoveScroll will inject props to this div
    Only this content would be scrollable
  </div>
</RemoveScroll> 
.fi
.PP
 Pick the first one if you don't need a second\&.
.SS "Position:fixed elements"
To properly size these elements please add a special className to them\&. 
.PP
.nf
import {RemoveScroll} from 'react\-remove\-scroll';

// to make "width: 100%"
<div className={cx(classWithPositionFixed, RemoveScroll\&.classNames\&.fullWidth)} />

// to make "right:0"
<div className={cx(classWithPositionFixed, RemoveScroll\&.classNames\&.zeroRight)} />

.fi
.PP
 See \fRreact-remove-scroll-bar\fP documentation for details\&.
.SS "More than one lock"
When stacked more is active (default) only one (last) component would be active\&.
.SS "Over isolation"
That could happen - you disable scroll on the body, you are suppressing all scroll and wheel events, and you are ghosting the rest of the page by the \fRinert\fP prop\&.

.PP
Only something inside Lock does exists for the browser, and that might be less than you expected\&.

.PP
Dont forget about \fRshard\fP, dont forget - \fRinert\fP is not portals friendly, dont forget - you dont need over isolation in most of the cases\&.

.PP
.RS 4
just be careful! 
.RE
.PP
.SH "Performance"
.PP
To do the job this library setup \fInon\fP passive event listener\&. Chrome dev tools would complain about it, as a performance no-op\&.

.PP
We have to use synchronous scroll/touch handler, and it may affect scrolling performance\&.

.PP
Consider using \fRnoIsolation\fP mode, if you have large scrollable areas\&.
.SH "Supported React versions"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
v1 supports React 15/16
.IP "\(bu" 2
v2 requires 16\&.8\&.0+ (hooks)
.PP
.SH "Scroll-Locky"
.PP
This is a refactoring of another library - \fRreact-scroll-locky\fP - to make package smaller and more react-portals friendly\&.
.SS "See also"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreact-focus-on\fP - Finite Modal creator (uses Scroll-Locky) underneath\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreact-locky\fP - React event canceler
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreact-scrolllock\fP - React scroll lock
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRscroll-lock\fP - DOM scroll lock
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbody-scroll-lock\fP - DOM scroll lock
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
This package is relative smaller(1), more react friendly(2), works with non zero body margins(3), and has a better "overscroll" management\&. 
.RE
